"Atoms in a Magnetic Field: teaching notes",
"Atoms in a Magnetic Field",
"Intro modern physics (atomic structure)",
"An orbiting electron is a current loop, so it has a magnetic moment. For any orbit the moment is tied to the angular momentum by μ = (e/2m_e) L, which makes μ_B = eħ/2m_e the natural unit.",
"The direction of L, and so of μ, is quantized: L_z = m_l ħ with m_l = −l, …, +l. In a field B each m_l has its own energy U = m_l μ_B B, so a level with 2l + {#} states splits into 2l + {#}.",
"The Stern–Gerlach experiment shows the quantization directly: a beam of silver atoms splits into two spots, not a smear. Two is an even number, which no orbital angular momentum can give (2l + {#} is odd): the moment is the electron's spin, with s = {#}/{#} and g ≈ {#}.",
"Spectral lines split in a magnetic field (the Zeeman effect). With no spin in the levels, a line splits into three evenly spaced lines; with spin, the pattern is more complicated.",
"Students should know the Bohr model (radius n²a₀, speed αc/n), the magnetic moment of a current loop μ = IA, and the quantum numbers n, l and m_l of the hydrogen atom. The demo has three views. <strong>Orbit</strong>: a Bohr orbit drawn as a current loop with its L and μ arrows, beside the quantum vector model (L on a cone about B) and a graph of the energy of each m_l level against B. <strong>Stern–Gerlach</strong>: atoms from an oven pass two slits, a magnet {#} cm long with a field gradient dB/dz and a drift space that can be set to zero, and stick to a glass screen; the lower panel shows the face of the screen and a count of atoms along z, with a cursor to measure positions. A velocity selector can pick one speed. <strong>Zeeman effect</strong>: a lamp between the poles of an electromagnet, the energy levels split into their m sublevels with every allowed transition, and the spectrum seen through a spectrometer that resolves {#} pm.",
"<strong>Orbit as a current loop.</strong> Ask Q1, then step n from {#} to {#}. The current falls fast (as {#}/n³) but the area grows as n⁴, so μ = IA grows as n; μ/L stays at {#} × {#}¹⁰ C/kg (Q2).",
"<strong>3d electron in a field.</strong> Five allowed directions of L. Step m_l and B and watch the fan of energy levels in the graph (Q3).",
"<strong>Stern and Gerlach, {#}.</strong> Ask what classical physics predicts before starting the beam (pick \"Classical magnets\" to show it). Silver gives two lips about {#} mm apart (Q4).",
"<strong>Silver at one speed.</strong> With the velocity selector at {#} m/s and the screen {#} cm past the magnet the spots are sharp. Step the gradient and measure the separation (Q5).",
"<strong>Mystery atom.</strong> Students count the spots, measure the spacing and find J and g (Q6).",
"<strong>Zeeman: cadmium red line.</strong> One line becomes three. Measure the spacing against B and get e/m_e (Q7). Then look along the field (the middle line disappears), and try the sodium D lines (Q8).",
